{"kind":"artifact","text":"Implementation at ui@3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200, cut from current main 72c3b724, fulfilling planner's request 54c40bf9 under my promise 68f51411. Successor to my candidate artifact 07123f67 at b60f3a40, which I am retiring.\n\nA DEVIATION YOU MUST RULE ON BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE. Your conditions name branch request/decision-records-ui. That branch is still occupied by the old head b60f3a40 — locally, in a worktree, and on origin. Rather than move or delete a ref that is the only pointer to the reviewed reference head, this work is on request/decision-records-ui-current. If you want the original branch name, say so and I will retire the old ref deliberately rather than quietly overwrite it. The head is what matters and it is exact; the branch name is not what I am asking you to accept silently.\n\nDEFECT 3, AUTHORITY, is the one that literally reproduces in main today. Toolbar.tsx:121 on main offers `agree` for every propose to any signed-in actor, and :114 offers `ratify yes`, with nothing reading roles. The fold refuses those: internal/workroom/fold.go:974-980 returns \"actor lacks ratifier role\" when the target kind's satisfier is role:X and the signer lacks it, and propose's satisfier is role:ratifier. So the UI has been offering acts the fold was always going to refuse, which teaches a person their authority only after telling them no.\n\nNew ui/src/lib/authority.ts mayRatify() implements the fold's rule from the fold's own published data rather than a copy of it: satisfier from the live vocabulary, roles from the projected roster. role:X requires holding X; originating-requester requires being that requester and still being in the roster; anything else is not ratifiable; and with no vocabulary it offers nothing rather than guessing. Toolbar gates agree, ratify yes and the report accept on it.\n\nI VERIFIED THIS MYSELF rather than reporting the implementing agent's account. At the head, npm test is 94 passing, 0 failing. I then replaced the mayRatify call with a function returning true — main's shape — and the test failed with exactly the right assertion: a role-less actor regained ['agree','disagree'] where ['disagree'] was expected. Tree restored byte-clean afterwards.\n\nDEFECT 2, CAP AND DETERMINISM. The kernel bound is real (internal/intent/intent.go:20, MaxCausalReferences = 4096, enforced at :87) and the ordering hazard is real: provenance arrives as a JSON object of arrays, whose order is emission order and not a contract. records.ts gains citableProposals(), which filters only on projected fields — kind propose, ratified, not retired, decision effective — sorts by fold sequence descending with event id breaking ties, then caps. Pinned by a test that reverses every input list and expects the same answer, and by a 500-candidate test for the cap.\n\nDEFECT 1, ADOPTION MEANING: I chose to document the contract rather than project a fact, and the reasoning is the part worth your attention. The fold projects no adoption relation — the resident's status projection carries decisions, acts, statements, commitments, reviews, artifacts, actors, provenance, opaque_kinds and omitted_supersessions, and nothing meaning \"an adoption that stands\". Consuming a projected adoption fact is therefore impossible, and producing one is a layer 5 change: a fold rule, a projected field and a schema bump, none of which this request covers or reviewed. Layer 7 already says ui/ must not infer application force the interpreter did not produce.\n\nSo the artifact row offers both acts unconditionally and gates neither on adoption. The reference head branched on inferred adoption; this one refuses to. A test pins that the prefill follows the fold's citation edge rather than a shared path, and gating the row on inferred adoption turns it red.\n\nDEFECT 4, MODAL FOCUS, on the real chain. New ui/src/lib/modalFocus.ts useModalFocus() gives initial focus, Tab and Shift+Tab wrapping, Escape dismissal and focus restoration to the opener, re-reading the focus ring on each keypress so a control that becomes enabled joins it.\n\nYour condition ruled out stubbing the seam and the test honours that: ui/test/publish-chain.test.mjs builds gs, git-inits a repository, runs gs init, spawns a real gs serve on a loopback port, mounts the real App in jsdom against it, and clicks the real publish button. The only adaptation is resolving relative fetch paths against the resident's address, because a jsdom document has no origin; request, transport, service and fold are the shipped ones. It asserts all four focus behaviours, submits, and reads the record back out of the fold's own projection, checking path, exact commit and verdict effective.\n\nONE HONEST NOTE ON WHAT \"REPRODUCES\" MEANS HERE. Defects 1, 2 and 4 concern a feature that is not on current main — there is no Publish.tsx, no adoption lookup, no artifact-row acts. They cannot reproduce as bugs in shipped code. What I confirmed still holds at current main are the conditions that make them defects: the absent adoption projection, the layer 7 prohibition, the real kernel bound, the unordered provenance wire shape. Defect 3 is different and reproduces literally. I would rather state that distinction than let a reviewer read four uniform reproductions into this.\n\nTESTS I CHANGED RATHER THAN ADDED, which deserve a reviewer's eye: two pre-existing cases encoded the defect — they modelled an empty actor map with no vocabulary and asserted ratify yes was offered. They now supply a ratifier and a vocabulary.\n\nGATES at this exact head: gofmt, go build ./... and go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... across the whole module, all packages ok; make ui-check green with the embed rebuilt and internal/service/uidist clean afterwards; git diff --check clean; worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200","path":"ui"}}